WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.968
- [Tyler] Three, two, one.

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.813
(cameras clicking)

00:00:03.813 --> 00:00:06.600
- This is one of the
first steps in a trend

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.480
that could upend the way movies are made.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.620
Her purse was full of useless trash!

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.750
AI gives Hollywood producers

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.270
the ability to capture a scan of a star

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.180
and use it to do, well,
pretty much anything.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.290
For example, neither of
these guys is the real me.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:29.310
But the technology used to
do that comes with big risks,

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.320
especially for actors.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.600
So I visited the CAA Vault.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.920
- [Jack] We'll just step you in.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.180
- [Erich] A studio created

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.580
by one of Hollywood's top talent agencies

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.880
to give actors control
of their digital doubles.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.960
- There's no way to imagine
a future world without this.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.480
- I don't quite know
what to anticipate here

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.304
but I'm excited to see what happens.

00:00:50.304 --> 00:00:51.180
(air whooshes)

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.150
The CAA Vault houses
four different stations

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:58.080
that record an actor's body,
face, movements, and voice.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.820
I feel like Jodie Foster in "Contact."

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.380
- Yeah.
- They should've sent a poet.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:02.370
Dominic Ridley,

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.580
the director of VFX company
Clear Angle Studios,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.490
works with CAA to create
these digital scans.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.040
- With shooting those 17
different lighting fires,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.890
we get to see how the light
is reacting to your materials

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:14.820
and your skin, et cetera.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.750
- The first step in the
process is a 3D full body scan.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.040
Why is this the pose?

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.600
- So this is basically just allowing us

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.360
to capture all the inside
of your arm and hands

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.520
while still being nice and relaxed.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.199
- It's hard not to be a little nervous.

00:01:28.199 --> 00:01:30.210
(cameras clicking)

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.162
- [Tyler] And that's it.

00:01:31.162 --> 00:01:33.450
1 1/2 seconds, that just
is your full body capture.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.560
- It's like a red carpet.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.500
It shoots 17 different
lighting environments

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.601
in just 1.2 seconds.

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:40.740
(cameras clicking)

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.310
Those few seconds capture
more than 200 images

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.750
that look like these.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.270
Later, all of the images will be used

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.910
in a process called photogrammetry,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.880
which brings together
hundreds of 2D images

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.470
in order to create a 3D image of my body.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:58.303
(air whooshes)

00:01:58.303 --> 00:02:01.710
The next step in the process
is a range of motion scan.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.720
- Once you start feeling
that kind of tension,

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.070
don't go too far past that.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:05.963
- Okay.

00:02:05.963 --> 00:02:07.050
It's just like following a workout video.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:07.890
- Yeah. Yeah, exactly.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.510
It's just like following a workout video.

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:11.070
- This is for all the beach scenes

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.110
you're filming me in.
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.040
- [Erich] As the technology

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.380
has grown more popular in the industry,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.050
actors are worried about
their digital doubles

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.810
being used without their consent.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.930
The use of AI doubles was
a major sticking point

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:28.980
in the recent union negotiations
between actors and studios.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.157
- [All] AI, bye-bye!

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.790
- I'd imagine AI is a bigger
and bigger part of your day.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.010
- Yeah, AI became something

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:43.010
that we as a company were
aware of a long time ago.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:46.320
- [Erich] Bryan Lourd is CEO
of the Creative Artists Agency

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.200
which represents actors like
Tom Hanks, Cate Blanchett,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.370
and Zendaya,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.860
and is the company beyond the CAA Vault.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.050
- What we believe is that an individual,

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.670
whether you're famous or not,

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.800
should own your digital presence

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.170
and the simple concept of the Vault

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.360
was to get out ahead of this.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.160
- [Erich] As the tech
becomes more accessible,

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.590
AI doubles could change
the way actors work.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.320
For example, they could
avoid hours in their trailer

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.700
thanks to digital makeup

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.710
or they could use their
double for reshoots

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:16.140
while they move on to another project.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.300
So far, there's been little protection

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.120
around how these digital doubles are used.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.690
There isn't any federal law
protecting this kind of likeness

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.420
and local laws vary from state to state.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.230
Many scans are owned
by studios, not actors.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.450
And payment is also an issue.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.420
How much should an actor make
when their double is used

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:37.740
instead of them?

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:41.130
Outside of work, anyone
can create their own scans

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.370
to make deepfakes that do or say anything.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.680
- I'm gonna show you some magic.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.960
- [Erich] The agency says the CAA Vault

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.700
is aimed at solving these issues

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.130
since all of the scans taken at the Vault

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.630
belong to the actors.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.340
So that means, at the end of the day,

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.090
my digital identity and all
of the scans used to create it

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.040
will belong to me.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.630
That includes my next one,

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.990
a face scan in a machine called Dorothy.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.880
- She encapsulates the highest technology

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.050
that we have at Clear Angle Studios

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.870
and in this system we're
gonna capture your face

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.040
in the highest detail,

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.010
both for a 3D scan, we can
do 4D scanning as well.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.010
- Do you feel like you're
living in the future?

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.690
- No, my wife keeps me very grounded.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.130
- Ah.
- Yep.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.860
- [Erich] It captures
the face in two sessions.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.470
The first for use with photogrammetry,

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.000
like in the full body scan.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.290
- [Jack] What this does
is it just allows very,

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:37.440
very high qualitative alignment

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.790
and when you're a 3D model,

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.530
we can stabilize your 3D models

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.210
around the cubes on your head.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.430
- [Erich] Each three second capture

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.490
grabs nearly 700 images.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.050
This is followed by a series

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:53.100
of 76 different facial expressions
with just a single flash.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.570
- [Jack] That was the
perfect outer brow raise

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.760
with this one, yeah.
- Oh, really?

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.030
The second stage is a
capture for generative AI,

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:01.650
which is a series of images

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.110
used by a machine learning platform

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.570
to recreate my image in 2D.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.420
This includes the full
spectrum of mouth movements,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.470
so my double can say any
word and appear authentic.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.210
To give the system enough options,

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.960
I had to express a lot of
emotions about a beige fox.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.960
The quick beige fox jumped in
the air over each thin dog.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.480
The lights flash in very
specific patterns in this scan

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.520
to simulate different environments.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.860
- And then we can replicate
the lighting from the set

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.710
in here and then redo the performance.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:37.320
- Those scans come out
looking something like this,

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.500
allowing my double to be placed
in different environments

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.620
without the lighting looking unnatural.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.920
These days, a chicken leg is a rare dish.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.980
Then, the last stage, a scan of my voice.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.260
I could try to do, like, a British accent.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:56.610
The hogs were fed
chopped corn and garbage.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:59.970
So why do you have me
saying these sentences?

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:01.170
Why do we care so much

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:05.730
about women with trash in
their purses and smoky flames?

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.340
- These are words and phrases

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.920
that will cover every possible sound

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.580
to make other phrases or words
happen, if that makes sense.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:15.630
- [Erich] So from this collection

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.850
of random sounds and sentences,

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.833
you can have me say or do anything?

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.740
- Yes. (laughs)

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.720
- CAA then stores the scans

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.650
and says they have a layer of
security to protect the data.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:32.430
Then I could hand off my
digital mask to VFX studios,

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.250
like CAA Vault's partner Deep Voodoo.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:39.870
They can then make a digital
rendering of, well, me.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:42.090
While the tech is a major talking point

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:43.470
in Hollywood right now,

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.830
CAA says it's not just
actors being scanned.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:50.370
Musicians, athletes,
directors, and content creators

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.740
have also gone through the process.

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:55.890
But there's a lot of uncertainty
about this technology.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:57.510
For example, it's unclear

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:59.310
how it could impact work in Hollywood

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.110
for people who aren't major stars,

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.720
or what its implications
might be in politics,

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:05.610
or our everyday lives.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:08.010
I feel like that when
you see this technology,

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:11.220
the first response is, "Oh
my god, this is so cool."

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.697
And the second response is,

00:07:12.697 --> 00:07:14.280
"This could ruin the world."
- "Oh my god."

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.240
Yeah, yeah.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.550
- Does it keep you up at night
and do you worry about that

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:21.870
as you try to in some ways
advance this technology?

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:22.703
- Yes.

00:07:22.703 --> 00:07:26.010
I mean, I think it was a
big fundamental question

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.337
that we had to ask ourselves is,

00:07:27.337 --> 00:07:29.340
"Do we bury our heads in the sand

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.430
or do we embrace this and go after it?"

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:36.630
And so we think we all have a
responsibility to embrace it,

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:40.110
but to embrace it with some
sort of ethics and morals

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.465
and fairness in mind.

00:07:42.465 --> 00:07:44.882
(soft music)

